{
  "requirement": "When a mandatory input or dispatched model read is denied by source-access\npolicy, the assessment MUST abort and report the denied path and reason to its caller.\nIt MUST NOT return the denial to the model as a recoverable tool result or make\nanother model request for that assessment. An untracked-file diagnostic MUST\nexplain that the caller can stage the file with `git add` or configure a workspace\nallow rule; the harness MUST NOT stage or permit the file itself.\n\nThe aborted assessment MUST NOT write evidence or emit evidence JSON. Its usage\nrecord MUST identify the failure and any failed tool call. In a batch, other\nassessments MUST continue, including queued assessments, and their successful\nwrites MUST be retained. The command MUST return a nonzero exit status after\nthe batch finishes. Discovery and search MAY omit denied candidates without\naborting, but explicitly requesting a denied file or directory MUST abort the\naffected assessment.\nMalformed tool arguments and ordinary read failures MUST retain their existing\nrecoverable tool-error behavior.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** Most of the requirement is implemented and well tested. One obligation is not met, and several implementing functions lack `{impl}` marks.\n\n**What is implemented and covered**\n- **Policy denials** are produced in `SourceAccess::path` and `PathPolicy::check_disallow` (`src/authoring/access.rs`). They carry `ErrorKind::PermissionDenied`, name the requested path, and give the reason.\n- **Untracked files** get a diagnostic saying `git add` or an explicit `habeas.toml` allow rule. Nothing in the harness stages files. `git_tools_allow_staged_and_dirty_sources_but_hide_untracked_files` and `mandatory_git_inputs_require_staging…` assert on \"git add\".\n- **Mandatory inputs** are read in `AssessmentSources::prepare` and `source_input` (`src/authoring/inputs.rs`). A denial returns before any provider request. This is covered by `disallowed_mandatory_inputs_abort_preparation` and `unreadable_or_denied_anchors_fail_before_inference`.\n- **Dispatched reads:** `assess` (`src/authoring/mod.rs`) returns the error at once when a tool fails with `PermissionDenied`, so there is no tool result for the model and no further request.\n  - The error then flows through `prepare_assessment` and `usage.finish(\"failed\")`, with `usage.tool(name, true)` recording the failed tool call.\n  - `tests/authoring_cli.rs::explicit_untracked_reads_abort_without_another_request_or_evidence_write` covers `habeas`, `ripgrep` and `list_files` with `--stdout`. It checks empty stdout, unchanged evidence, `request_count == 1`, `tool_failures[tool] == 1` and `evidence_written == false`.\n- **Batch behaviour** works because `cancel_on_error` cancels only on `Interrupted`, so a denial does not stop the batch. `tests/authoring_batch.rs` covers this in `denied_read_aborts_only_its_assessment_and_runs_queued_assessments` and `denied_read_preserves_in_flight_assessments_and_their_evidence`. Together they check that queued and in-flight assessments finish, their evidence is kept, and the command exits nonzero.\n- **Discovery and search** skip denied candidates, while explicit requests abort. `list_files`, `search` and the walker do this, and the tests in `tools.rs` and `access.rs` cover it.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **Ordinary read failures are not kept recoverable.** The abort decision in `assess` is `error.kind() == io::ErrorKind::PermissionDenied`. That kind is also what the OS reports for ordinary permission errors (`EACCES`).\n   - `resolve` in `access.rs` re-wraps the `canonicalize` error with `io::Error::new(error.kind(), …)`.\n   - `list_files` does the same for `read_dir` and `metadata` errors, and `prepare` for anchor reads.\n   - An unreadable file or directory under OS permissions would therefore abort the whole assessment. The requirement says ordinary read failures MUST keep their recoverable tool-error behaviour.\n   - Policy denial and OS failure share one error kind. No test covers the distinction. The only recoverable-failure check is the missing-file case (`absent.txt`) in `ollama_cli_delivers_sources…`.\n2. **Incomplete `{impl}` marking.** These functions enforce or propagate access denial for dispatched reads, but are not marked `{impl}` for `read_denial`:\n   - `tools::dispatch` and `AssessmentSources::read_selector`.\n   - `AssessmentSources::read_lines` and `additional_definitions`.\n   - The `references` tool, which implements the \"discovery MAY omit denied candidates\" part.\n3. **Test gaps.**\n   - No end-to-end test shows a mandatory-input denial producing the usage failure record or the batch behaviour. Only the unit-level `prepare` error is tested.\n   - The CLI untracked test asserts only that stderr contains \"untracked\". That substring is matched by the file names themselves, not by the \"git add / allow rule\" wording, which is checked only in unit tests.\n\nNo FIXME or issue references acknowledge these gaps, so the result is `false` rather than `FIXME`.",
